<section class="section section-tight">
  <div class="container two-col-grid">
    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">The analytics engineer premium</h2>
      <p>Analytics engineer is not just a rebranding of data engineer — it represents a specific skill profile: dbt expertise, semantic layer ownership, and close collaboration with business stakeholders to build production-grade data models. At companies with mature modern data stacks, analytics engineers own assets that directly drive business decisions — and their comp reflects that ownership.</p>
      <p>If your work involves dbt, building data marts, defining metrics, and owning the semantic layer, you should be benchmarking against analytics engineer comp at comparable companies — not general DE averages, which include lower-paying ETL/pipeline roles.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">How data engineers leave money on the table</h2>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Wrong title benchmark</strong> — Comparing against general DE averages instead of the specific title (analytics engineer, data platform engineer, ML platform engineer) that matches your work</li>
        <li><strong>Underselling infrastructure impact</strong> — Pipeline reliability, query cost reduction, and data freshness SLAs have direct dollar values that most DEs never quantify in their resume or interviews</li>
        <li><strong>Not pushing on equity</strong> — At Tier-1 companies, DE equity grants are comparable to SWE grants at the same level; enterprise DE offers often have room to negotiate equity where base may be fixed</li>
        <li><strong>Ignoring the SWE comp benchmark</strong> — At top companies, senior DEs earn within 10–15% of senior SWEs; if you are being offered significantly less, the comp is not calibrated correctly</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container faq-stage">
    <div class="faq-header">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Data engineer salary — common questions</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-grid">
      <details class="faq-card" open>
        <summary>What is a good data engineer salary in 2026?</summary>
        <p>For a senior data engineer at a Tier-1 tech company, $210K–$340K total comp is the 2026 benchmark. At enterprise companies, $155K–$225K is typical at the same level. "Good" salary varies significantly by company tier — the same senior DE role pays 40–60% more at FAANG than at an enterprise company, almost entirely due to equity differences.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-card">
        <summary>Do data engineers earn as much as software engineers?</summary>
        <p>At Tier-1 tech companies, senior data engineers and senior software engineers earn comparable total compensation — typically within 10–15% of each other at the same level. At enterprise and mid-size companies, the gap is larger: enterprise SWE comp often exceeds enterprise DE comp by 15–25% due to higher demand. As the modern data stack has matured, the DE/SWE comp gap at top companies has narrowed significantly.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-card">
        <summary>What is an analytics engineer salary compared to a data engineer?</summary>
        <p>Analytics engineers at mid-size and enterprise companies often earn 5–15% more than traditional data engineers at the same experience level — reflecting higher strategic proximity to business decisions. At Tier-1 tech companies, the difference is minimal as both are benchmarked against SWE comp bands. The bigger factor is company tier: a senior analytics engineer at a top company earns more than a principal data engineer at a mid-size enterprise.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-card">
        <summary>How do I negotiate a higher data engineer salary?</summary>
        <p>Quantify your infrastructure impact before entering any negotiation: pipeline reliability (uptime SLA), query cost reduction (dollar amount), data freshness improvements (time reduction), and downstream business decisions enabled. These translate directly to business value and are far stronger negotiating anchors than "built pipelines" or "improved data quality." Then use Levels.fyi to anchor your ask to verified comp data for your level and company tier.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

